{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"e204c39fb3188371a12272b21b490102a90d1f4ba0f1b9c4418517e14e842c2b","slug":"ch02.index","location":"/ch02/index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Chapter 2: Getting Started","content_includes_title":false,"github":"https://github.com/jpowerj/clrs","source_url":"https://github.com/jpowerj/clrs/blob/main/ch02/index.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/jpowerj/clrs/edit/main/ch02/index.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"index.md","url":"/index-5b6068d2e95001762074c13f3729d5e6.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This chapter will familiarize you with the framework we’ll use throughout the book to think about the design and analysis of algorithms. It is self-contained, but it does include several references to material that will be introduced in Chapters 3 and 4. (It also contains several summations, which Appendix A shows how to solve.)","position":{"start":{"line":4,"column":1},"end":{"line":4,"column":1}},"key":"JDuZOGJQFr"}],"key":"EjyhDbKrss"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"We’ll begin by examining the insertion sort algorithm to solve the sorting problem introduced in Chapter 1. We’ll specify algorithms using a pseudocode that should be understandable to you if you have done computer programming. We’ll see why insertion sort correctly sorts and analyze its running time. The analysis introduces a notation that describes how running time increases with the number of items to be sorted. Following a discussion of insertion sort, we’ll use a method called divide-and-conquer to develop a sorting algorithm called merge sort. We’ll end with an analysis of merge sort’s running time.","position":{"start":{"line":6,"column":1},"end":{"line":6,"column":1}},"key":"OxJ5rga